          "node": {
            "body": "\u003cp\u003eHi Product Hunt, I'm William! I made Rill because I want to always stay in the flow. \u003cbr\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAs an ADHD, I never liked code-switching. My best ideas for work come when I'm supposedly not working at all: watching something for fun, reading things and following a rabbit hole, looking at how someone else solved a problem. Then when an idea hit me, I'd have to stop, switch to coding agents, find the project and explain it all, and most of the time I just didn't.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eRill is my way of removing that code-switching. It's a browser with your Claude Code or Codex working alongside you. When something on a page gives you an idea, you say so right there, and an agent starts on it in the right project. You don't have to spend time looking for the right folder, rephrasing everything you just saw on the web, and you don't switch in and out of work mode. The work goes on with you in the flow.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThis matters now more than ever. Agents have shortened the distance between ideas to output near zero. So the bottleneck is capturing the idea at the moment you have it. That moment is nearly always while you're taking something in, and that is what a browser is for.\u003cbr\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eRill is a free AI-native browser. It's WebKit-based, which means it's native to Mac. And it runs on the Claude Code or Codex plan you already have, so you don't have to buy a new subscription for all of its agentic capabilities.c\u003cbr\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eHow do you keep an idea today when it shows up at the wrong time?\u003c/p\u003e",
            "createdAt": "2026-10-05T07:00:22Z",
            "id": "5920840",
            "parentId": null,
            "replies": {
              "edges": [
